{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778942236,"nonce":"SSYww+52iYLcrYFb3EgDKfXl9aU=","message":"## Implementation design v0: shadow reconciliation and cutover gates\n\nThis issue owns the migration safety layer. It should make ledger adoption observable and reversible instead of switching the system from git-bug scanner to ledger in one jump.\n\n### Principle\n\nDo not create a deployed state where Mandible writes to the ledger but cannot mirror/export to git-bug and cannot show conflicts. Read cutover and write cutover are separate gates.\n\n### Runtime modes\n\nAdd explicit settings or config-backed modes. Suggested settings:\n\n- `ledger.issues.read_mode`: `scanner`, `shadow`, `ledger`.\n- `ledger.issues.write_mode`: `git_bug`, `dual`, `ledger`.\n- `ledger.issues.shadow_report_enabled`: boolean.\n- `ledger.issues.fallback_on_ledger_error`: boolean, default true during migration.\n\nMode behavior:\n\n- `scanner`: existing IssueScanner behavior; ledger may import in background only.\n- `shadow`: responses still come from scanner, but ledger projection is computed and compared; mismatches are stored/reported.\n- `ledger`: responses come from ledger; scanner remains available for fallback/manual comparison.\n- `git_bug` writes: existing direct git-bug mutation path.\n- `dual` writes: ledger command plus git-bug/outbox path with strict result tracking; exact behavior depends on adapter readiness.\n- `ledger` writes: ledger command is canonical; git-bug export is outbox/retry/conflict managed.\n\nDo not enable `ledger` write mode before the adapter issue provides outbox processing, external cursors, and conflict records.\n\n### Reconciliation report shape\n\nCreate a typed reconciliation output, probably persisted in a table or emitted to logs/API. It should be queryable from tests and UI.\n\nSuggested record fields:\n\n- `id`\n- `project_id`\n- `checkout_id` or `work_repo_id`\n- `repo_display`\n- `started_at`, `finished_at`\n- `status`: `running`, `clean`, `mismatch`, `failed`\n- `scanner_issue_count`, `ledger_issue_count`\n- `scanner_open_count`, `ledger_open_count`\n- `scanner_closed_count`, `ledger_closed_count`\n- `mismatch_count`\n- `error`\n- `summary_json`\n\nSuggested mismatch entries:\n\n- missing in ledger;\n- extra in ledger;\n- ambiguous alias/prefix;\n- title mismatch;\n- body hash mismatch;\n- status mismatch;\n- label set mismatch;\n- comment count mismatch;\n- comment hash mismatch;\n- parent/child mismatch;\n- auth link missing;\n- work-unit link missing;\n- session link missing.\n\nThe report should include enough IDs/aliases to investigate without opening raw DB rows.\n\n### Alias and prefix policy\n\nDuring migration, any short reference can be:\n\n- exact canonical ID;\n- exact alias in one project;\n- unambiguous alias prefix in one project;\n- ambiguous across projects;\n- ambiguous within a project;\n- not found.\n\nAPI/CLI behavior should distinguish ambiguity from not-found. Do not silently pick the first match. For routes that include repo/project context, use that context to disambiguate; for global commands, require explicit project/repo when a prefix collides.\n\nCommit trailer parsing remains compatible with `Bug: \u003cshort-id\u003e`, but the migration layer must resolve it through project context when possible and surface ambiguous trailers as actionable errors or reconciliation warnings.\n\n### Existing route compatibility\n\nKeep current response shapes where the UI and CLI depend on them. `types.ts` currently defines git-bug-shaped issue interfaces; early cutover can map ledger rows into compatible shapes while adding ledger-specific fields under optional `detail`/metadata fields or new endpoints.\n\nExisting endpoints to cover:\n\n- `GET /api/issues`\n- `GET /api/issues/find/:issueId`\n- `GET /api/issues/:repoId/:issueId`\n- `POST /api/issues/:repoId/:issueId/status`\n- `POST /api/issues/:repoId/:issueId/comment`\n- `POST /api/issues/:repoId/:issueId/label`\n- `POST /api/issues/scan`\n- `POST /api/issues/:repoId/scan`\n- `GET /api/issues/:repoId/:issueId/context`\n- `GET /api/issues/:repoId/:issueId/sessions`\n- `POST /api/issues/:repoId/:issueId/spawn`\n- issue resolution endpoints.\n\nFor scan endpoints, once ledger is authoritative, the operation should trigger adapter import/reconciliation rather than synchronous scanner refresh, but keep the endpoint contract stable during migration.\n\n### Cutover sequence\n\nRecommended implementation order inside this issue:\n\n1. Add read-mode plumbing with `scanner` as default.\n2. Add ledger-to-legacy response mappers.\n3. Add reconciliation runner comparing scanner output to ledger projections.\n4. Add `/api/issues/reconcile` or equivalent diagnostic endpoint if useful.\n5. Add shadow mode: serve scanner, compare ledger, record mismatches.\n6. Fix/backfill until shadow is clean on Mandible's real repos.\n7. Add ledger read mode with fallback to scanner on configured errors.\n8. Add write-mode guards but leave `ledger` writes disabled unless adapter gates are satisfied.\n9. Switch writes only after adapter outbox/conflict handling is tested.\n\n### Rollback path\n\nRollback must be simple:\n\n- set read mode back to `scanner`;\n- set write mode back to `git_bug`;\n- leave ledger tables intact for diagnosis;\n- do not delete imported ledger records automatically;\n- if dual/ledger writes created outbox rows, leave them visible and retryable/conflicted rather than dropping them.\n\n### Performance gates\n\nUse concrete targets rather than vague improvement claims. Initial targets can be adjusted after measurement, but the issue should produce measurements for:\n\n- `/api/issues` with current real corpus;\n- `/api/issues/:repo/:issue` detail;\n- context endpoint;\n- reconciliation run on Mandible repo;\n- import run on Mandible repo.\n\nImportant behavioral target: ledger-backed read routes must not spawn `git-bug` on the request hot path.\n\n### Tests\n\nRequired tests:\n\n- mapper tests from ledger rows to existing `GitBugIssue` / detail-ish API shape;\n- alias resolver tests including ambiguity;\n- reconciliation tests for clean corpora and each mismatch class;\n- mode-switch tests proving scanner/shadow/ledger read behavior;\n- fallback tests when ledger read throws or data is missing;\n- route tests preserving existing endpoint response contracts;\n- write-mode guard tests preventing ledger-only writes before adapter readiness;\n- commit trailer resolution tests with project context and ambiguity.\n\n### Done check\n\nAfter this issue, Mandible should have a proven path to switch issue reads and writes to the ledger without data loss, silent ID collision, or stale git-bug divergence. The default may still remain conservative until the user explicitly approves cutover.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}